      <p>Pest control searches are specific and emotional. Nobody searches for "pest control" in general when there is a rat in the loft; they search for exactly what they found. Google Ads works best when the account mirrors that: by pest, by season and by the value of the customer each search tends to bring.</p>

      <h2>Build campaigns around pests, not the industry</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Rodents:</strong> mice and rats, often year-round with peaks as the weather cools</li>
        <li><strong>Stinging insects:</strong> wasps, hornets and bees, urgent and strongly seasonal</li>
        <li><strong>Crawling insects:</strong> ants, cockroaches, bed bugs, each with different urgency and job value</li>
        <li><strong>Termites and wood-destroying insects</strong>, where relevant in your market, usually high value with inspections first</li>
        <li><strong>Commercial pest control</strong> for restaurants, warehouses and offices, with contracts rather than one-off visits</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Ads that name the exact pest win more clicks than generic pest control ads, and separate campaigns let budget follow the season.</p>

      <h2>Move budget with the seasons</h2>
      <p>Pest demand rises and falls through the year, and each pest has its own calendar. Insect searches climb in warm months; rodent searches tend to rise as temperatures drop. Rather than one flat budget, shift spend between pest campaigns as the seasons change, so money goes where the searches are.</p>

      <h2>A protection plan is worth more than a visit</h2>
      <p>The most valuable pest control customer is not the one-off treatment, it is the household or business that signs up for a recurring plan. If every booking counts as one equal conversion, the account cannot tell them apart.</p>
      <p>Give plan sign-ups a higher conversion value than single treatments, and feed that back from your booking or CRM system. Bidding then favours the searches that produce long-term customers. The <a href="/tools/metrics-calculator/">metrics calculator</a> helps work out what you can pay for each.</p>

      <h2>Keep DIY searches out</h2>
      <p>Pest control has one of the largest DIY audiences of any home service. Many searchers want a spray, a trap or a video, not a technician. These are the main sources of waste:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Products: spray, traps, poison, bait, repellent</li>
        <li>How-to searches: how to get rid of ants, how to kill wasps yourself</li>
        <li>Job and licence searches from people entering the trade</li>
        <li>Pests you do not treat, and areas you do not serve</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The <a href="/tools/negative-keyword-generator/">negative keyword generator</a> builds a starter list, and <a href="/learn/how-to-find-negative-keywords/">how to find negative keywords</a> explains how to keep it current.</p>

      <h2>Discretion sells</h2>
      <p>Many customers, especially restaurants and hotels, care about discretion as much as results: unmarked vehicles, quiet visits, documentation for inspections. Where these are true for you, they make strong ad callouts and landing page points, because they answer worries the searcher may not type.</p>

      <h2>Local Services Ads and Search</h2>
      <p>Where Local Services Ads are available, they appear above the normal results and charge per lead. They suit urgent residential calls. Search gives control over which pests and services you appear for, which matters for termites, commercial contracts and plans. Measure the two separately. <a href="/learn/local-services-ads-vs-google-ads/">Local Services Ads vs Google Ads</a> covers the differences.</p>

      <h2>Where to start</h2>
      <p>If you already advertise, the first question is whether the account knows which bookings became recurring plans. A <a href="/google-ads-audit/">$100 account analysis</a> checks tracking and where the budget goes by pest. <a href="#enquiry">Ask for a free audit</a> and tell me which pests and services you most want more of.</p>
